-- 1183: Blog post - "Strawberries Grown Indoors in Singapore: Inside Today's
--       Hydroponics Class"
--
--       Tenth post for WSQ - Basic Urban Farming with Hydroponics (TGS-2025053916).
--       TODAY's class (29 Aug 2026) at MEOD Farm, led by Dr Samita, built around the
--       indoor STRAWBERRY vertical farm (admin request).
--       Cross-links to five sibling posts.
--
-- *** THIS POST IS CURRENT-DATED, NOT BACK-DATED. ***
-- Unlike 1162/1163/1165/1166/1169/1172/1174/1175/1177, `linkedin_urn` is left NULL
-- so the post CAN be shared to LinkedIn (admin asked for it). The manual-skip
-- sentinel rule applies only to back-dated posts - see the blog-pipeline skill.
-- The share is triggered explicitly on prod AFTER the hero exists:
-- Mage::getModel('mmd_blog/cron_autoblog')->shareEverywhere($post). shareEverywhere()
-- passes hero_image_url to the LinkedIn helper, and a share with no image 422s
-- (see feedback_linkedin_share_must_have_image), so hero FIRST, share SECOND.
--
-- Every course figure was read off the LIVE course page on 2026-08-29: $350 before
-- GST, GST $31.50 (9%), nett $206.50 / $136.50, 1 day / 8 hrs at MEOD Farm, funding
-- valid 24 Mar 2025 - 23 Mar 2027, TSC AGR-FOP-2002-1.1.
--
-- VENUE: the admin wrote "Meow farm". The live course page says "MEOD Farm 13 Neo
-- Tiew Harvest Ln" and the farm guide's polo in one photograph reads "MEO...", so
-- the copy uses MEOD Farm. This is the CURRENT course venue, so unlike the historical
-- posts there is no past/present venue split to manage.
--
-- TRAINER: named as "Dr Samita" exactly as the admin gave it. No credentials,
-- affiliation or specialisation are asserted - we have none. One photograph shows a
-- farm guide in a green MEOD polo, but she is NOT identified as Dr Samita in the copy
-- because nothing confirms that, and the figcaption says only "a farm guide".
--
-- STRAWBERRY CLAIMS are standard controlled-environment-agriculture horticulture
-- (temperate crop, day-length response, humidity/fungal risk, no indoor pollinators)
-- and are stated qualitatively. NO yield, temperature setpoint, variety name, energy
-- figure or production volume is asserted - the photographs do not show them and we
-- have no source for this farm's operation. "Several thousand" describes what is
-- visible in the wide rack photograph.
--
-- Photographs: 7 of 8 supplied. Note the seedling-tray photo shows CORIANDER and
-- leafy greens, NOT strawberries, so its figcaption says "propagation trays" without
-- claiming strawberry seedlings. Sources 1600-2048px native, downscaled to 950px q58:
-- 795KB total, avg 113KB, all verified HTTP 200. Under blog/hydroponics/2026-08-29-*.
--
-- Notes for future editors:
--  * apply.php splits statements on ";" at end-of-line - the content HTML is ONE line.
--  * Content is pure ASCII; excerpt uses a REAL em-dash via CHAR() (see 1161);
--    meta_description carries NO em-dash entity (see 1164).
--  * FAQ ships as native <details> accordions per the blog-pipeline rules.
--  * Idempotent: WHERE NOT EXISTS on url_key, INSERT IGNORE on tag links.
--  * SG-only guard via @mms_instance.
--  * hero_image_url is NOT set here - generate on prod post-deploy BEFORE sharing.

SET @is_sg := IF(@mms_instance = 'SG', 1, 0);


INSERT INTO `mmd_blog_post` (`title`, `url_key`, `excerpt`, `content`, `author`, `status`, `published_at`, `related_skus`, `source_sku`, `meta_title`, `meta_description`, `meta_keywords`, `created_at`, `updated_at`) SELECT 'Strawberries Grown Indoors in Singapore: Inside Today''s Hydroponics Class', 'strawberry-indoor-hydroponics-farm-singapore-2026', CONCAT('Strawberries are a temperate crop. Singapore is on the equator. Today our class stood in front of a vertical farm growing them indoors under pink LEDs ', CHAR(0xE2,0x80,0x94 USING utf8mb4), ' no winter, no imports, no soil. Here is how that works, and what it takes.'), '<p>Strawberries want a cool climate and a winter chill. Singapore sits one degree off the equator and has neither. For most of the country''s history, a strawberry here has been something that arrived on a plane.</p> <p>Today our <a href="https://www.tertiarycourses.com.sg/wsq-basic-urban-farming-with-hydroponics.html">WSQ - Basic Urban Farming with Hydroponics</a> class stood in front of several thousand of them, growing indoors, in Singapore.</p> <figure class="mmd-blog-figure"> <img src="https://pub-77c0dec029944b0386e40673ce81081f.r2.dev/blog/hydroponics/2026-08-29-strawberry-vertical-farm-led.jpg" alt="Class participants looking through a viewing window at a large indoor vertical farm growing strawberries on stacked tiers under pink LED lighting" /> <figcaption>The moment the room went quiet: tier after tier of strawberries under LED, seen through the viewing window at MEOD Farm. Photo: Tertiary Infotech Academy.</figcaption> </figure> <p>The class of <strong>29 August 2026</strong> was led by <strong>Dr Samita</strong> at <strong>MEOD Farm</strong>, and this is what the day covered.</p> <h2>Why strawberries are the hard case</h2> <p>Leafy greens are the easy win in hydroponics &mdash; fast, shallow-rooted, forgiving. Strawberries are the opposite, and that is exactly why growing them well is such a good demonstration of what controlled-environment agriculture can do.</p> <p>A strawberry plant asks for things Singapore''s outdoor climate cannot provide:</p> <ul> <li><strong>Cool temperatures.</strong> Strawberries are a temperate crop. Sustained tropical heat pushes them into stress rather than fruiting.</li> <li><strong>A controlled day length.</strong> Flowering in many varieties responds to how long the light lasts, and equatorial days are close to twelve hours year-round with no seasonal swing.</li> <li><strong>Low humidity around the fruit.</strong> Warm, humid air is ideal for the fungal diseases that ruin soft fruit.</li> <li><strong>Pollination.</strong> Indoors there is no wind and there are no insects, so fruit set is something a grower has to arrange.</li> </ul> <p>Grow them in a sealed indoor environment and every one of those becomes a setting rather than a constraint. That is the whole argument for this kind of farming, made in the most demanding way available.</p> <h2>Inside the growing room</h2> <figure class="mmd-blog-figure"> <img src="https://pub-77c0dec029944b0386e40673ce81081f.r2.dev/blog/hydroponics/2026-08-29-strawberry-nursery-racks.jpg" alt="The class gathered beside a tall multi-tier rack of young strawberry plants lit by pink LED bars, next to a door marked Nursery 3" /> <figcaption>Strawberry racks in the nursery under LED bars. Every tier has its own light. Photo: Tertiary Infotech Academy.</figcaption> </figure> <p>Look at how the racks are built. Each tier carries its own LED bar, because in a stacked system the shelf above is the sky for the shelf below &mdash; there is no sunlight reaching down through five levels. Every layer has to be lit independently, and that is what makes vertical farming an electricity business as much as an agriculture one.</p> <p>The light is pink for the same reason grow lights always are: chlorophyll absorbs strongly in the red and blue bands and reflects most green. A lamp built to emit mainly red and blue delivers what the plant uses and skips paying for what it reflects away.</p> <figure class="mmd-blog-figure"> <img src="https://pub-77c0dec029944b0386e40673ce81081f.r2.dev/blog/hydroponics/2026-08-29-indoor-growing-racks.jpg" alt="Long indoor growing racks holding trays of leafy vegetables under lighting, running the length of a climate-controlled room" /> <figcaption>Indoor growing racks running the length of the room. Photo: Tertiary Infotech Academy.</figcaption> </figure> <figure class="mmd-blog-figure"> <img src="https://pub-77c0dec029944b0386e40673ce81081f.r2.dev/blog/hydroponics/2026-08-29-leafy-greens-channels.jpg" alt="Dense beds of leafy greens growing in evenly spaced holes across white hydroponic channel boards" /> <figcaption>Leafy greens on channel boards &mdash; the bread and butter alongside the strawberries. Photo: Tertiary Infotech Academy.</figcaption> </figure> <p>The greens matter commercially even when the strawberries get the attention. They are fast, reliable and sell continuously, which is what funds the harder, slower, higher-value crop growing in the next room.</p> <h2>It all starts in the nursery</h2> <figure class="mmd-blog-figure"> <img src="https://pub-77c0dec029944b0386e40673ce81081f.r2.dev/blog/hydroponics/2026-08-29-seedling-nursery-trays.jpg" alt="Close view of propagation trays filled with young coriander and leafy vegetable seedlings in individual plugs" /> <figcaption>Propagation trays: every plant on the farm starts here, one plug at a time. Photo: Tertiary Infotech Academy.</figcaption> </figure> <p>Whatever the crop, the sequence is identical: germinate in a plug, grow on in the nursery, transplant into the production system. A farm seeds in staggered batches so the growing rooms fill continuously and the harvest never stops &mdash; the scheduling discipline that separates supplying food from merely growing it.</p> <figure class="mmd-blog-figure"> <img src="https://pub-77c0dec029944b0386e40673ce81081f.r2.dev/blog/hydroponics/2026-08-29-farm-walkthrough.jpg" alt="A farm guide in a green polo with a headset explaining the growing system to class participants beside a plastic-curtained growing area" /> <figcaption>The walkthrough. Questions asked in front of a working system get better answers. Photo: Tertiary Infotech Academy.</figcaption> </figure> <h2>What indoor strawberries actually require</h2> <p>Strip out the novelty and the requirements are a checklist any serious grower will recognise:</p> <ol> <li><strong>Temperature control</strong>, which in Singapore means active cooling and the energy bill that comes with it.</li> <li><strong>A light recipe and a schedule</strong> &mdash; intensity, spectrum and day length, held consistent by a timer rather than by memory.</li> <li><strong>Humidity and airflow management</strong>, because soft fruit in warm, still, humid air is an invitation to fungal disease.</li> <li><strong>Hand or assisted pollination</strong>, since nothing indoors will do it for you.</li> <li><strong>A nutrient recipe that shifts by stage</strong>, from leafy growth into flowering and fruiting.</li> <li><strong>Measurement, constantly.</strong> pH and EC still decide whether the plant can use what is in the tank, exactly as they do in a jam jar.</li> </ol> <p>That last point is the thread running through the whole course. A vertical farm is a tabletop hydroponics kit with better engineering and stricter bookkeeping. The physics does not change with scale.</p> <h2>Today''s class</h2> <figure class="mmd-blog-figure"> <img src="https://pub-77c0dec029944b0386e40673ce81081f.r2.dev/blog/hydroponics/2026-08-29-class-group-photo.jpg" alt="The 29 August 2026 hydroponics class posing together in the training room, two participants holding bags of seedlings they are taking home" /> <figcaption>The 29 August 2026 class, with seedlings going home. Photo: Tertiary Infotech Academy.</figcaption> </figure> <p>Everyone left with seedlings and a system they had put together themselves. That is the point of the day: you cannot run a vertical farm after eight hours, but you can leave understanding exactly what one is doing and why &mdash; and with something growing on your own windowsill to practise on.</p> <h2>The course</h2> <p><a href="https://www.tertiarycourses.com.sg/wsq-basic-urban-farming-with-hydroponics.html">WSQ - Basic Urban Farming with Hydroponics</a> (course code TGS-2025053916) is a <strong>1-day, 8-hour</strong> class at Beginner level with a 1-hour assessment, held at <strong>MEOD Farm, 13 Neo Tiew Harvest Lane</strong>. It maps to the SkillsFuture Skills Framework TSC <strong>Urban Farming Implementation and Management Control (AGR-FOP-2002-1.1)</strong>, with two learning outcomes:</p> <ol> <li>Perform farming activities and equipment operations according to standard operating procedures</li> <li>Execute farming operations work in accordance with regulations, WSH practices and housekeeping rules</li> </ol> <p>The day covers hydroponic system types (deep water culture, nutrient film technique, wick, ebb and flow, drip), growing media, plant nutrition and nutrient solutions, pH and EC monitoring, indoor farming conditions including lighting, temperature and humidity control, Singapore''s urban farming regulations, good agriculture practices and workplace safety &mdash; plus hands-on practice with a hydroponics kit and a guided tour of a commercial hydroponics farm.</p> <h3>What it costs</h3> <p>The full fee is <strong>$350</strong> before GST. With WSQ funding:</p> <table> <tr><th>Who you are</th><th>Nett fee</th><th>Funding</th></tr> <tr><td>Singapore Citizen / PR, aged 21 and above</td><td><strong>$206.50</strong></td><td>50% course-fee funding</td></tr> <tr><td>Singapore Citizen aged 40 and above (MCES), or SME-sponsored</td><td><strong>$136.50</strong></td><td>70% course-fee funding</td></tr> </table> <p>GST of $31.50 (9%) applies to the full course fee. Funding is valid <strong>from 24 March 2025 to 23 March 2027</strong>. Eligible Singapore Citizens can apply <strong>SkillsFuture Credit</strong> and <strong>PSEA</strong> against the nett fee, NTUC members may claim <strong>UTAP</strong>, and employers can tap <strong>SFEC</strong> with <strong>Absentee Payroll</strong> support. Completion &mdash; at least 75% attendance plus a pass in the assessment &mdash; earns a Certificate of Achievement from Tertiary Infotech Academy and an <strong>OpenCert (Statement of Achievement) from SkillsFuture Singapore</strong>.</p> <p><a href="https://www.tertiarycourses.com.sg/wsq-basic-urban-farming-with-hydroponics.html"><strong>Check the next class date and funding eligibility &rarr;</strong></a></p> <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2> <div class="mmd-faq"> <details class="mmd-faq-item"> <summary class="mmd-faq-q">Can strawberries really be grown in Singapore?</summary> <div class="mmd-faq-a"><p>Not outdoors in the open, but yes in a controlled indoor environment. Strawberries are a temperate crop that wants cool temperatures, a managed day length and low humidity around the fruit. Indoors under LEDs with active cooling, each of those becomes a setting you control rather than a climate you are stuck with.</p></div> </details> <details class="mmd-faq-item"> <summary class="mmd-faq-q">Why is the light pink?</summary> <div class="mmd-faq-a"><p>Because it combines red and blue diodes. Chlorophyll absorbs strongly in the red and blue bands and reflects most green, which is why leaves look green. A lamp emitting mainly red and blue gives the plant the wavelengths it uses without paying to produce light it reflects away.</p></div> </details> <details class="mmd-faq-item"> <summary class="mmd-faq-q">How are indoor strawberries pollinated?</summary> <div class="mmd-faq-a"><p>Not by wind or insects, because indoors there are neither. Fruit set has to be arranged by the grower, whether by hand, by air movement, or by introducing pollinators deliberately. It is one of the costs of a sealed environment that people rarely think about until they see the flowers.</p></div> </details> <details class="mmd-faq-item"> <summary class="mmd-faq-q">Does the course teach me to grow strawberries?</summary> <div class="mmd-faq-a"><p>The course teaches the foundations that any crop rests on &mdash; system types, growing media, nutrient solutions, pH and EC control, and indoor conditions including lighting, temperature and humidity. The strawberry farm is the guided-tour half of the day: it shows what those fundamentals look like at commercial scale and at the difficult end of the crop range.</p></div> </details> <details class="mmd-faq-item"> <summary class="mmd-faq-q">What should a beginner grow first?</summary> <div class="mmd-faq-a"><p>Leafy greens and herbs &mdash; lettuce, bok choy, kailan, basil. They are fast and forgiving, so you complete several crop cycles, and several mistakes, in the time a single fruiting crop would take. Work up to fruit once you can hold a stable pH and EC through a full cycle.</p></div> </details> <details class="mmd-faq-item"> <summary class="mmd-faq-q">Is this course funded?</summary> <div class="mmd-faq-a"><p>Yes. WSQ funding brings the $350 fee to $206.50 for Singapore Citizens and PRs aged 21 and above, or $136.50 for Singapore Citizens aged 40 and above under MCES. SkillsFuture Credit, PSEA and UTAP can be applied on top. Funding runs from 24 March 2025 to 23 March 2027.</p></div> </details> </div> <h2>Where to go next</h2> <p>A strawberry growing a degree off the equator is a good reminder of what this technology actually removes: the assumption that where you are decides what you can grow.</p> <ol> <li>Start with leafy greens on a shelf and get through one complete crop cycle.</li> <li>Join the next <a href="https://www.tertiarycourses.com.sg/wsq-basic-urban-farming-with-hydroponics.html"><strong>WSQ - Basic Urban Farming with Hydroponics</strong></a> class &mdash; from <strong>$136.50 nett</strong> for eligible Singapore Citizens aged 40 and above &mdash; and see the strawberry farm for yourself.</li> <li>Keep a log of pH, EC and light hours. Every serious grower you meet keeps one.</li> </ol> <p>More from this course: <a href="https://www.tertiarycourses.com.sg/blog/indoor-urban-farming-led-grow-lights-singapore-2018">why grow lights are pink</a>, <a href="https://www.tertiarycourses.com.sg/blog/hydroponics-jar-of-water-windowsill-singapore-july-2025">hydroponics in a jam jar</a>, <a href="https://www.tertiarycourses.com.sg/blog/hydroponics-kit-practical-build-singapore-december-2025">building a deep water culture kit</a>, <a href="https://www.tertiarycourses.com.sg/blog/hydroponics-grow-melons-singapore-class-october-2025">growing melons hydroponically</a>, and <a href="https://www.tertiarycourses.com.sg/blog/urban-farming-with-hydroponics-guide">how hydroponics works</a>.</p>', 'Tertiary Infotech Academy', 1, '2026-08-29', 'TGS-2025053916', 'TGS-2025053916', 'Strawberries Grown Indoors in Singapore | WSQ Hydroponics Course', 'Strawberries are a temperate crop and Singapore is on the equator, yet they grow here indoors under LEDs. What indoor strawberry farming requires, from our 29 August 2026 hydroponics class at MEOD Farm. WSQ funded from $136.50 nett.', 'indoor strawberry farming singapore, strawberries hydroponics, vertical farming singapore, led grow lights, controlled environment agriculture, meod farm, hydroponics course singapore, wsq urban farming, skillsfuture hydroponics, indoor farming singapore', NOW(), NOW() FROM DUAL WHERE @is_sg > 0 AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM (SELECT `post_id` FROM `mmd_blog_post` WHERE `url_key` = 'strawberry-indoor-hydroponics-farm-singapore-2026') x);

